<section class="page-head">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="page-head__eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>From the Army to enterprise SEO.</h1>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:var(--space-m)">
      I bridge the gap between what search engines require and what front-end
      engineering can actually deliver.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--flush">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="hero__grid">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I came to digital marketing the long way around. I learned to work hard
          as a young person, applied it in the <strong>United States Army</strong>,
          then spent time delivering and installing window coverings before going
          back to school on the GI Bill.
        </p>
        <p>
          That turned into an Associate of Arts from Clark College on the Vice
          President's Honor Roll, and then a
          <strong>BA in Digital Technology and Culture</strong> from Washington
          State University Vancouver in 2018 &mdash; Summa Cum Laude, 3.96 GPA.
          That program is where I first learned SEO, and I've been doing it
          professionally ever since.
        </p>
        <p>
          Since 2018 I've worked at <strong>Audigy</strong>, a fast-paced marketing
          agency serving hearing healthcare practices and B2B partners. I started
          building websites, moved into SEO, and now own technical, local, and
          content SEO strategy across a portfolio of more than 110 client sites.
          Most recently I led the modernization of the SEO service itself &mdash;
          taking it from a monthly blogging and listings program to a strategic
          offering with its own positioning, pricing, enablement guides, and
          annual client strategy sessions.
        </p>
        <p>
          The thread through all of it: I don't treat SEO as keywords and metadata.
          I look under the hood &mdash; site architecture, CMS migrations, Core Web
          Vitals, measurement plumbing &mdash; and then translate what I find into
          language a practice owner or a marketing manager can act on.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        {% include "partials/hero-photo.njk" %}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Experience</h2>
    <ol class="timeline" style="margin-top:var(--space-l)">
      {%- for role in career.experience %}
      <li class="role">
        <div class="role__head">
          <div>
            <h3 class="role__title">{{ role.title }}</h3>
            <p class="role__org">{{ role.company }} &middot; {{ role.location }}</p>
          </div>
          <p class="role__dates">{{ role.startLabel }} &ndash; {{ role.endLabel }}</p>
        </div>
        <p class="role__summary">{{ role.summary }}</p>
        <ul class="role__bullets">
          {%- for bullet in role.bullets %}
          <li>
            <h4>{{ bullet.heading }}</h4>
            <p>{{ bullet.text }}</p>
          </li>
          {%- endfor %}
        </ul>
      </li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--alt">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Education &amp; service</h2>
    <ul class="cards" style="margin-top:var(--space-l)">
      {%- for school in career.education %}
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="card__title">{{ school.credential }}</h3>
        <p class="muted">{{ school.institution }} &middot; {{ school.year }}</p>
        <p class="card__result">{{ school.honors }}</p>
        {%- if school.note %}<p class="muted">{{ school.note }}</p>{% endif %}
      </li>
      {%- endfor %}
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="card__title">{{ career.military.branch }}</h3>
        <p class="muted">{{ career.military.note }}</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap wrap--narrow stack">
    <h2>Tools I work in daily</h2>
    <ul class="tags">
      {%- for tool in capabilities.tools %}
      <li class="tag">{{ tool }}</li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/case-studies/">See the work</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="{{ site.resume }}">Download r&eacute;sum&eacute; (PDF)</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
